  "justification": "The original Stage 3 architecture cannot meet peak demand: two t3.micro instances (40 req/s each = 80 req/s total) fall far short of the 750 req/s origin requirement (N1). The proposed architecture scales to t3.medium instances (160 req/s each at 70% CPU). To sustain 750 req/s, we need ⌈750/160⌉ = 5 instances at peak. The design maintains a minimum of 2 instances across 2 AZs (N2), scales to 5 during the 60 monthly peak hours, and maintains 3 instances baseline, averaging 3.4 instances monthly. RDS db.t3.medium Multi-AZ enables automatic failover (N2) with 7-day backup retention, achieving the 5-minute RPO and 1-hour RTO (N3) via continuous Multi-AZ replication and automated point-in-time recovery. Total cost: $747.27, within the $750 budget (N5). The design addresses all five AWS Well-Architected Framework pillars: **Operational Excellence** through automated Auto Scaling policies that respond to demand changes and RDS Multi-AZ failover automation, reducing manual intervention; **Security** via private subnets isolating compute from internet exposure, ALB in public subnets as sole entry point, VPC security groups restricting traffic; **Reliability** through redundant instances across 2 Availability Zones, Multi-AZ RDS with synchronous replication and automatic failover, CloudFront distribution caching content, Auto Scaling replacing unhealthy instances; **Performance Efficiency** by right-sizing t3.medium instances to the minimum class meeting peak load (800 req/s capacity), using Auto Scaling to adjust capacity dynamically from baseline to peak, avoiding over-provisioning; **Cost Optimization** by implementing baseline/peak scaling to pay only when needed, single NAT Gateway reducing infrastructure costs, t3-generation burstable instances reducing idle-hour overhead.",
